---
title: Brined Pork Roast with Port Wine Sauce
servings: 12
prepTime: 25 hr
cookTime: 1.5 hr
totalTime: 1d + 1.5hr
difficulty: Medium
Rating: 8/10
description: Very moist and tender with pork loin ends. Great as leftovers.
source: Taste of Home (dead link)
tags: Alcohol, Pork
---

> Note: Port wine sauce can be made 1 day ahead. Cover and refrigerate. Reheat over low when ready to serve.

= Brine (24 Hours) =
In a Dutch oven, combine the @water{8%cups}, @kosher salt{1/2%cup}, @brown sugar{1/2%cup},
@fresh thyme{6%sprigs}, @fresh rosemary{3%sprigs}, @fresh sage{3%sprigs}, @bay leaves{3},
@fennel seed{1%Tbsp}, @coriander seeds{1%Tbsp}, and @whole peppercorns{1%Tbsp}.
Bring to a boil. Cook and stir until salt and sugar are dissolved.
Remove from the heat, cool brine to room temperature.

Place a 2-gallon resealable plastic bag inside another resealable bag; add @pork loin roast{4%lb}(boneless).
Carefully pour cooled brine into bag.
Squeeze out as much air as possible; seal bags and turn to coat.
Place in a large bowl.
Refrigerate for ~{18-24%hours}, turning occasionally.

= Roast =
Drain and discard brine; pat roast dry.
In a small bowl, combine the @olive oil{1%Tbsp}, @marjoram{2%tsp}, @fresh rosemary{2%tsp},
@fresh sage{2%tsp}, @fresh thyme{2%tsp}, and @pepper{1/2%tsp}; rub over pork.

Place roast on a rack in a large shallow roasting pan.
Pour @water{1%cup} into pan.
Bake at 350°F for ~{75%min} or until a thermometer reaches 145°F.

= Sauce =
About half way through the roast cooking time, start making the sauce.
Melt @butter{1/4%cup}(cubed) in a large saucepan.
Add @shallots{3}(thinly sliced); cook and stir until tender.

Stir in @port wine{1.5%cups} and @bay leaf{1}.
Bring to a boil; cook until liquid is reduced by half.

Stir in the @chicken broth{14.5%oz}(low-sodium), @white wine vinegar{2%Tbsp}, and @sugar{5%tsp}.
Cook, uncovered, for ~{20-25%minutes} or until slightly thickened.

Stir in the @heavy whipping cream{1/4%cup}, @fresh thyme{1.5%tsp}(minced), @salt{1/8%tsp},
@pepper{1/8%tsp}, and @?browning sauce{1/2%tsp}(optional).
Discard bay leaf.

Combine @cornstarch{2%tsp} and @cold water{2%tsp} until smooth; gradually stir into the pan.
Bring to a boil; cook and stir for 2 minutes or until thickened.

Transfer pork to a platter. Stand 10 minutes before slicing.
